# Fonts
# The `font` command's roster, ungated by machine role, because a client machine wants
# readable code too. Every family named in dot_config/font/registry.json comes from a cask
# here, or the switch writes a family macOS silently substitutes for and you find out from
# a terminal full of replacement boxes. Fonts live in homebrew/cask now; the old
# homebrew/cask-fonts tap was folded in, so no tap line is needed.
#
# Cask names don't track family names, and the near misses are plausible enough to install
# without noticing: font-monaspice-* yields MonaspiceNe, font-caskaydia-cove-* yields
# CaskaydiaCove, and Maple's -nl casks mean "no ligatures", which would quietly defeat the
# one thing every font on this list was picked for. See _working-memory/dataContracts.md.

# Tier A, patched Nerd Font statics. These are what the terminals use: icons come baked in,
# so there's no fallback chain to get wrong. Two of them cover two roster keys each, since
# monaspice ships Neon and Xenon and recursive-mono ships Casual and Linear.
cask "font-caskaydia-cove-nerd-font"
cask "font-fantasque-sans-mono-nerd-font"
cask "font-lilex-nerd-font"
cask "font-maple-mono-nf"
cask "font-monaspice-nerd-font"
cask "font-recursive-mono-nerd-font"
cask "font-victor-mono-nerd-font"

# Tier B, the unpatched variable builds VS Code uses instead. The patcher can't round-trip
# fvar/gvar, so patched-or-variable is a real either/or rather than a preference; the axes
# are worth having in the editor and worth nothing in Ghostty. Icons come from the
# symbols-only family below, which every Tier B editor entry names as its fallback.
cask "font-cascadia-code"
cask "font-lilex"
cask "font-maple-mono"
cask "font-monaspace-var"
cask "font-recursive"
cask "font-symbols-only-nerd-font"

